{"title":"ASX set for bright start despite Wall Street slip; $A drops (The Sydney Morning Herald, 2023-12-05T21:51:55+00:00)","text":"ASX set for bright start despite Wall Street slip; $A drops (The Sydney Morning Herald, 2023-12-05T21:51:55+00:00)
Too much strength would give inflation more fuel, but too little would mean a recession.

With inflation down from its peak two summers ago, Wall Street is hopeful that the Federal Reserve may finally be done with its market-shaking hikes to interest rates and could soon turn to cutting them. That could help the economy avoid a recession and give a boost to all kinds of investment prices. Investors have been looking for a slowdown in the job market in particular. The hope is that it can cool more through employers cutting back on open positions than on employers laying off workers. Tuesday’s report showed that employers advertised just 8.7 million jobs on the last day of October, down by 617,000 from a month earlier. In the bond market, US Treasury yields continued to sag further from the heights they reached during late October. The yield on the 10-year Treasury fell to 4.18 per cent from 4.26 per cent late on Monday, offering more breathing space for stocks and other markets.","entities":["$A","ASX set for bright start despite Wall Street slip; $A drops","US Treasury","Federal Reserve","The Sydney Morning Herald","stocks","bond market","interest rates","recession","ASX","Wall Street","job market","inflation"],"facts":["ASX set to open higher as Wall Street rebounds; $A rises -MENTIONS-> $A","ASX set for bright start despite Wall Street slip; $A drops -MENTIONS-> Utilities and energy stocks","ASX set for bright start despite Wall Street slip; $A drops -MENTIONS-> Perpetual","ASX set for bright start despite Wall Street slip; $A drops -MENTIONS-> Endeavour","ASX set for bright start despite Wall Street slip; $A drops -MENTIONS-> Evolution Mining"]}

